The head is **tagged** (§5.3, §7.2). Without the tag the text is not an anchor at
all — `plugin-markdown` links it and the extras head stays literal (§14): no
`type`, no `class`.

```text
@[Inner text](/docs "Head title")@@anchorA{.hero}
```

@[Tagged](/docs)
[Untagged](/docs)@@anchorA{.hero}

§6.5: the preset assigns `bracket.bare → inner_text`, `paren.bare → href` and
`paren.quote → title`. The other three positions of the same head are bare fields
under their slot-qualified names — kept, never dropped (D-7).

@[Inner "Head quote" `Head tick`](/docs "Head title" `Paren tick`)

The tag is read wherever a head starts, because an anchor is inline by nature:

Inline: see @[the docs](/docs) for details.

§6.2: the construct head wins over the extras, and the extras value is dropped.

@[Head title wins](/docs "Head title")@@anchorA{"Extras title"}

`#id` beats the extras slug, and an extras slug becomes the id when nothing else
did (§6.2).

@[Explicit id](/docs)@@anchorA{`slug-extras`, #explicit}

@[Slug only](/docs)@@anchorA{`slug-only`}

`href` is owned by the link; the extras value is an `Error` that names
`field.override.href` (§13, D-12 rule 5).

@[Href is owned](/docs)@@anchorA{href: "/evil"}

§14 (D-16): automatic external-link attributes and the URL-suffix modifiers are
gone. A URL is a URL — no `target`, no `rel` — and `^`, `!`, `$`, `;;`, `--` are
ordinary characters now:

@[No external defaults](https://example.com/bar)
@[Suffix is text](/docs^!$;;--)

`target` and `rel` are ordinary extras keys now (§6.3):

@[Extras attributes](https://example.com/bar)@@anchorA{target: "_self", rel: "prefetch", hreflang: "en"}

A marker with no configured entry keeps the built-in `<a>` element and carries
the marker as its `type` attribute (§11 rule 3):

@[Unclaimed marker](/docs)@@anchorZ{.hero}

@[No marker at all](/docs)
